== Normalized concept dossiers ==

=== Machine learning ===
Machine learning studies computational methods that improve or adapt task performance using data or experience rather than relying only on explicitly hand-written task rules.

The field includes supervised, unsupervised, self-supervised, reinforcement, online, generative, representation, and other learning paradigms.

'''Do not conflate:''' machine learning ≠ AI as a whole; machine learning ≠ biological learning; fitting a statistical model can be machine learning, but statistical inference is broader.

=== Generalization (machine learning) ===
Generalization is a learned system's ability to achieve useful performance on new examples or conditions beyond the specific examples used to fit it.

Classical generalization normally concerns unseen samples from the same or intended target data-generating process.

Out-of-distribution performance is a stronger/different question and should not be silently folded into the base definition.

'''Do not conflate:''' generalization ≠ benchmark score; low training error is not sufficient evidence of generalization.

=== Overfitting ===
Overfitting occurs when a learning procedure adapts to idiosyncrasies, noise, or finite-sample details such that predictive performance on unseen target data is worse than the fitted/training performance would suggest.

Overfitting is a relationship among learner, data, objective, and evaluation regime.

Modern overparameterized models complicate the old rule “more parameters → more overfitting”; highly parameterized interpolating models can still generalize well.

'''Do not conflate:''' overfitting ≠ memorization by definition; memorization and generalization can coexist.

=== Underfitting ===
Underfitting occurs when the learned predictor fails to capture relevant structure needed for good performance, often yielding poor fit even on training data and weak generalization.

Causes can include insufficient representational capacity, excessive regularization, inadequate optimization, poor features, or an inappropriate hypothesis class.

'''Do not conflate:''' underfitting ≠ simply “small model”.

=== Inductive bias ===
Inductive bias is the set of assumptions, preferences, constraints, or prior structure that allows a learner to choose among hypotheses that fit observed data and to make predictions about unseen data.

Examples include architectural structure, regularization, priors, invariances, simplicity preferences, locality, smoothness, and optimization-induced preferences.

'''Do not conflate:''' inductive bias ≠ cognitive bias; it is not necessarily an error.

=== Bias–variance tradeoff ===
Bias–variance analysis decomposes expected prediction error under specified settings into components associated with systematic error, sensitivity to training-sample variation, and irreducible noise.

The classical tradeoff is useful, but the simple monotonic story “more complexity → lower bias/higher variance → one U-shaped optimum” is not universal in modern overparameterized regimes.

Double-descent results show that test error can decrease again beyond interpolation in some model/data regimes.

'''Do not conflate:''' estimator bias here ≠ cognitive bias or unfairness.

=== Training set ===
A training set is the data used directly to fit model parameters or representations.

Training data can influence learned parameters through gradient updates, likelihood optimization, rule induction, retrieval construction, or other fitting procedures.

'''Do not conflate:''' training set ≠ every datum available during development.

=== Validation set ===
A validation set is held out from parameter fitting and used during development to select models, hyperparameters, stopping points, preprocessing choices, thresholds, or other design decisions.

Repeated validation use can itself be overfit.

'''Do not conflate:''' validation set ≠ final unbiased test set.

=== Test set ===
A test set is held out from model development and used to estimate performance after choices have been fixed.

If test results repeatedly influence model design, the test set effectively becomes part of the development loop and its estimate becomes optimistic.

'''Do not conflate:''' “test” is a role, not a permanently special dataset independent of how it is used.

=== Distribution shift ===
Distribution shift occurs when the data-generating distribution relevant at evaluation/deployment differs from the training distribution.

Dataset shift can involve changes in input distribution, label distribution, conditional relationships, environments, or combinations.

Covariate shift is one special case in which the input distribution changes while the conditional target relationship is assumed stable.

'''Do not conflate:''' distribution shift ≠ overfitting; a perfectly fit in-distribution model can fail after the environment changes.

=== Data leakage (machine learning) ===
Machine-learning data leakage occurs when information unavailable under the intended prediction/evaluation conditions enters training, feature construction, preprocessing, validation, or model selection in a way that yields misleading performance.

Examples include target leakage, future/temporal information, preprocessing before splitting, duplicate/group overlap, train–test contamination, and indirect identifiers.

'''Do not conflate:''' this is not the same concept as security data exfiltration.

=== Benchmark contamination ===
Benchmark contamination occurs when benchmark evaluation items, answers, close derivatives, or benchmark-specific information influence training or tuning so that benchmark performance no longer provides clean evidence of independent generalization.

It is a special evaluation-contamination problem and can arise through pretraining data, supervised tuning, synthetic-data pipelines, benchmark-specific optimization, repeated exposure, or indirect derivative data.

'''Do not conflate:''' benchmark contamination ≠ every benchmark flaw; poor construct validity or saturation can occur without contamination.

=== Model selection bias ===
Model selection bias occurs when a model/hyperparameter/configuration is selected because it performs best on a noisy finite-sample selection criterion, causing the selected score to overestimate future performance.

Repeated search over many alternatives increases opportunities to overfit the selection criterion.

'''Do not conflate:''' model-selection bias ≠ sampling selection bias and ≠ parameter overfitting only.

=== Spurious correlation ===
A spurious correlation is an observed association that does not reflect the stable task-relevant relationship one intends to learn and can break under intervention, environmental change, subgroup shift, or new data.

In machine learning, spurious feature–label correlations can support high in-distribution accuracy while damaging robustness/generalization.

'''Do not conflate:''' not every noncausal correlation is useless; a noncausal predictor can remain stable and useful under a specified deployment distribution.

=== Shortcut learning ===
Shortcut learning occurs when a learner relies on comparatively easy predictive cues or decision rules that succeed on the training/benchmark distribution but fail to capture the intended task-relevant strategy and therefore break under challenging conditions.

Shortcuts often exploit spurious correlations, artifacts, backgrounds, acquisition signatures, lexical cues, or benchmark regularities.

'''Do not conflate:''' spurious correlation describes a data/relation property; shortcut learning describes learner behavior that exploits such cues.

== Modern evidence corrections ==

=== Bias–variance and double descent ===
Classical bias–variance analysis remains valid under its assumptions, but modern overparameterized learning invalidates a simplistic universal complexity curve.

Belkin et al. (2019) showed a double-descent risk curve in which increasing capacity can first worsen and then improve test performance beyond the interpolation threshold.

The Core page must preserve both the classical decomposition and this limitation.

=== Model-selection overfitting ===
Cawley & Talbot (2010) show that model-selection criteria such as cross-validation estimates can themselves be overfit; selection bias can be comparable in magnitude to differences between algorithms.

This supports treating [[Model selection bias]] as distinct from ordinary model-parameter overfitting.

=== Distribution shift ===
The Dataset Shift literature distinguishes general joint-distribution shift from special cases such as covariate shift.

The Core page must not define all distribution shift as covariate shift.

=== Shortcut learning ===
Geirhos et al. (2020) define shortcut learning around decision rules that work on standard benchmarks but fail under more challenging conditions.

Recent work continues to study spurious-feature and shortcut dependence, so the page should treat shortcut learning as an active research framing, not as a theorem explaining every generalization failure.

=== Benchmark contamination ===
Benchmark contamination has become especially visible in large-model evaluation, but the concept is broader than LLMs.

Current contamination-free/live-benchmark work illustrates the evaluation problem without making LLM benchmarking the definition.

